    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's dive into the technical aspects of DNS propagation and caching. When you update the A records for your primary service, the new IP address is propagated across the DNS system. This propagation involves several steps and players, including authoritative name servers, recursive resolvers, and caching mechanisms.\n\n1. **Authoritative Name Servers**: When you make a change to your DNS records, the authoritative name servers for your domain are the first to receive this update. They update their records and then propagate these changes to other name servers, which are part of the DNS hierarchy.\n\n2. **Recursive Resolvers**: These are the DNS servers that clients query to resolve domain names to IP addresses. When a recursive resolver receives a query, it checks its cache to see if it has the most recent information. If it doesn't, it will query the authoritative name servers to get the updated information.\n\n3. **TTL (Time to Live)**: The TTL value in your DNS records tells recursive resolvers how long to cache the information before checking back with the authoritative servers for updates. A lower TTL means the information is refreshed more frequently, while a higher TTL means it can be cached for a longer period.\n\n4. **Cache Hierarchy**:",
      "source": "target",
